{title: Abide with Me}
{artist: Henry Francis Lyte (1793-1847)}
{key: Eb}
{copyright: Public Domain}

{sov: Verse 1}
A[Eb]bide with me; fast [Bb]falls the even[Cm]tide;
The [Ab]darkness deepens; [Eb]Lord, with me a[Bb]bide.
When [Eb]other helpers [Bb]fail and comforts [Cm]flee,
Help [Ab]of the helpless, [Bb]O abide with [Eb]me.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 2}
Swift [Eb]to its close ebbs [Bb]out life’s little [Cm]day;
Earth’s [Ab]joys grow dim, its [Eb]glories pass a[Bb]way;
Change [Eb]and decay in [Bb]all around I [Cm]see;
O [Ab]Thou who changest [Bb]not, abide with [Eb]me.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 3}
I [Eb]need Thy presence [Bb]every passing [Cm]hour;
What [Ab]but Thy grace can [Eb]foil the tempter’s [Bb]power?
Who, [Eb]like Thyself, my [Bb]guide and stay can [Cm]be?
Through [Ab]cloud and sunshine, [Bb]Lord, abide with [Eb]me.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 4}
Hold [Eb]Thou Thy cross be[Bb]fore my closing [Cm]eyes;
Shine [Ab]through the gloom and [Eb]point me to the [Bb]skies;
Heav’n’s [Eb]morning breaks, and [Bb]earth’s vain shadows [Cm]flee;
In [Ab]life, in death, O [Bb]Lord, abide with [Eb]me.
{eov}
